<Base
	title="About"
	description="Werner Altewischer — MSc in applied physics, software architect, CTO at Beatgrid."
>
	<h1>About</h1>

	<p>
		Behind Media is the company of <strong>Werner Altewischer</strong> — MSc in
		applied physics, software architect, and currently Chief Technology Officer at{' '}
		<a href="https://beatgrid.co">Beatgrid</a>, where we measure cross-media
		advertising using passive audio content recognition.
	</p>

	<p>A bit of background.</p>

	<p>
		I graduated from Delft University of Technology in 2002, in the Pattern
		Recognition and Image Processing group of the Applied Physics faculty, working
		on soccer-playing robots in a team of around ten students. As part of that
		graduation project we competed in the RoboCup World Championships in 2001. My
		thesis was on implementing robot soccer player skills using vision-based
		motion. During my studies I already worked part time in the software industry,
		mostly writing Visual Basic and C++ for a proprietary CMS.
	</p>

	<p>
		After graduating I worked as a Java engineer and architect. I started at Nuon
		on the server back-end, and in 2004 moved to MarketXS, a company specialised in
		distributing real-time financial market data. There I architected and built
		UserXS, their entitlement, session management and reporting system. MarketXS
		was acquired by Markit in 2006.
	</p>

	<p>
		In August 2007 I joined former colleagues in founding Layerstream, a startup
		working on advanced technology for real-time video streaming. We set up a US
		entity in Silicon Valley in January 2008 and looked for VC investment there. I
		was largely responsible for the video coding software and its integration with
		the network components.
	</p>

	<p>
		In July 2008 I returned to the Netherlands and started working freelance,
		mostly on iPhone and Java server-side development. Over the following decade I
		built apps for companies ranging from small to multinational — among them
		Greetz, EveryTrail, KLM, Ahold and ING. EveryTrail was acquired by TripAdvisor
		in 2010. On the side I developed BehindTheFrontDoor, a talent platform based on
		media sharing, which went live in October 2012; it never reached the critical
		mass to sustain itself.
	</p>

	<p>
		I joined Beatgrid in March 2020, and am now its CTO. We run a single-source panel that
		measures advertising exposure across TV, CTV, online and out-of-home, using
		passive automatic content recognition on mobile devices. My work spans the
		whole stack: the C++ signal-processing engine that does the audio
		fingerprinting and watermarking, the iOS and Android clients that have to run
		it continuously without ruining anyone's battery or privacy, the JVM back-end,
		and the infrastructure underneath all of it.
	</p>

	<p>
		It took eighteen years, but it turns out I went back to pattern recognition — the
		same problem I graduated on, pointed at audio instead of video.
	</p>

	<h2>What I work with</h2>

	<p>
		C++, Kotlin, Java, Swift and Objective-C. Digital signal processing, real-time
		and concurrent systems, mobile architecture on both platforms, scalable server
		applications, and the build and release infrastructure that keeps all of it
		shippable. A strong theoretical foundation from physics and mathematics, which
		I use more often than I expected to.
	</p>

	<h2>Open source</h2>

	<p>
		I wrote <a href="https://github.com/werner77/WEPopover">WEPopover</a>, a generic
		popover implementation for iOS, which has collected around 1,200 stars and is
		still in use. There is also{' '}
		<a href="https://github.com/werner77/BMCommons">BMCommons</a>, an iOS
		foundation library, and a few smaller tools. I have solved{' '}
		<a href="https://github.com/werner77/AdventOfCode">Advent of Code</a> in Kotlin
		every year since 2015.
	</p>

	<h2>Away from the keyboard</h2>

	<p>
		Besides programming I do a lot of sport — mostly cycling, these days gravel
		racing, and I still ride competitively in my age category. I like being
		outdoors, and occasionally I play some chess.
	</p>
</Base>
